From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 07:00:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] let a frame source that a device drives capture a whole
 request

The emulated sensor pulls: for each buffer of a request it asks its frame source to paint a frame
for a time the sensor chose. A frame source that stands for a camera outside the HAL cannot work that
way. The camera exposes once for all the streams of a request, the shutter time is the time of that
exposure, and the picture, its metadata and a JPEG's Exif data come from the device.

Add an optional, host-driven mode to IFrameSource. For such a source the sensor queues every output
buffer of the request in one call (BeginRequest), collects them one by one (ProduceFrame, or
ProduceBlob for a JPEG), reads the frame time and the exposure's own values from FrameResult, sends the
shutter message itself and returns the buffers. A source that does not opt in is driven exactly as
before. The request's controls that only such a source needs (the modes and triggers of 3A, the white
balance gains, the flash mode, the JPEG quality and the metering regions) are carried to it in a
HostSettings member of the sensor settings.

The sensor's limits are widened to what a host-driven camera can offer: frame durations from 4 ms, a
sensitivity range of ISO 1 to 102400, and any of the four Bayer orders. An idle sensor, with no
request, keeps its 33 ms cycle. A flush or a close interrupts the waits of a capture in progress
(IFrameSource::Interrupt), so that they do not hold the thread up for the device's own timeout; the
sensor takes the cancellation back (ClearInterrupt) under the control mutex when it takes the next
request, so that a flush between that and the first wait still counts. A result reports the neutral
color point that the source measured when it has one.

A BLOB output that is not a JFIF picture, or has no result metadata, is not queued: the sensor does
not collect it, and a buffer the source cannot take back must not be handed to the device. The
settings also carry the top of the request's AE target frame rate range (HostSettings::ae_max_fps).

A request none of whose outputs was produced failed as a whole. The sensor asks the source
(IFrameSource::FailsRequest) and, when the source says so, reports ERROR_REQUEST for the request,
with no shutter message and no result metadata, and returns every buffer marked as failed, as the
stock request processor does for the pending requests that a flush drops. Only that tells an
application that its capture failed (onCaptureFailed); an ERROR_BUFFER alone does not, and a request
that lost only some of its outputs still reports ERROR_BUFFER for those.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
